---
import Presentation from "../../../../components/Presentation.astro";
import Divider from "../../../../components/ui/Divider.astro";
import Layout from "../../../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { Code } from "astro-expressive-code/components";

//#region CodeSnippets
const Strong = `A STRONG PASSWORD

  "something that is easy for a user to remember
   but would be difficult for someone else to guess
   BASED ON KNOWLEDGE OF THAT USER"

Not "hard for a computer to guess."
Hard for someone who KNOWS things about you.`;

const Factors = `MULTIFACTOR AUTHENTICATION

  at least TWO of these three categories

    KNOWLEDGE    something you know
    POSSESSION   something you have
    INHERENCE    something you are

  Each step adds a layer that must be broken
  to gain unauthorized access.`;

const Two = `TWO PASSWORDS IS NOT TWO FACTORS

  password + security question    both KNOWLEDGE
                                  -> one factor

  password + code to your phone   knowledge
                                  + possession
                                  -> two factors`;

const Encryption = `ENCRYPTION    encoding data to prevent
              unauthorized access

DECRYPTION    decoding it

SYMMETRIC KEY
  one key, used for both
  both sides must keep the same secret

PUBLIC KEY  (asymmetric)
  a PUBLIC key encrypts
  a PRIVATE key decrypts

  the sender does not need the receiver's
  private key to encrypt a message`;

const Excluded = `WHAT IS NOT ON YOUR EXAM

  "Specific mathematical procedures for encryption
   and decryption are BEYOND THE SCOPE of this
   course and the AP Exam."

No ciphers. No modular arithmetic. No key math.

What IS examinable: what each approach is FOR,
and which key does which job.`;

const Certificates = `CERTIFICATE AUTHORITIES

  issue digital certificates that VALIDATE THE
  OWNERSHIP of encryption keys used in secure
  communications

  and are BASED ON A TRUST MODEL

Encryption proves a message was not read.
A certificate is how you know whose key it was.`;
//#endregion
---

<Layout>
  <div class="container my-4">
    <div class="row justify-content-center">
      <div class="col-sm-12 col-md-10">
        <a href="/courses/ap-csp/" class="btn btn-sm btn-outline-light mb-3">
          <i class="si-arrow-left"></i> Back
        </a>

        <h1>Activity 3.17: Authentication and Encryption</h1>
        <Divider variant="8" classes="mb-5" />

        <!-- Brief -->
        <section id="brief">
          <h2>Two Different Questions</h2>

          <p>
            <b>Authentication asks: are you who you say you are?</b>
            <b class="text-sn-theme"> Encryption asks: can anyone else read this?</b>
          </p>

          <p>
            They are constantly confused, they solve different problems, and the exam
            tests both.
          </p>

          <Code code={Excluded} frame="code" lang="txt" title="Read This First" showLineNumbers={false} class="my-4" />

          <p>
            <b>You will not be computing anything today.</b> That is not a
            simplification &mdash; it is an explicit exclusion in the framework.
          </p>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Launch / Concept -->
        <section id="launch">
          <Presentation>
            <section>
              <h2>Activity 3.17</h2>
              <h3 class="text-sn-theme">Authentication and Encryption</h3>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Key Concepts</h2>
              <p>What Makes a Password Strong</p>
              <p>Two Factors, Not Two Passwords</p>
              <p>Which Key Does Which Job</p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Authentication</h2>
              <p><b>Measures that protect devices and information from unauthorized access.</b></p>
              <p class="fragment">Examples the exam names: <b>strong passwords</b> and <b>multifactor authentication</b>.</p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>What Makes a Password Strong</h2>
              <Code code={Strong} frame="code" lang="txt" title="Read the Last Line" showLineNumbers={false} />
              <p class="fragment"><b class="text-sn-theme">Based on knowledge of that user.</b></p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Which Changes the Advice</h2>
              <p>Your birthday is a weak password. So is your dog's name.</p>
              <p class="fragment">Not because they are short. <b>Because someone who knows you can guess them.</b></p>
              <p class="fragment">
                And session 75 was about how much a stranger can find out about you.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>PII Feeds This Directly</h2>
              <p>A weak password is one that <b>PII can predict</b>.</p>
              <p class="fragment">Your birthday, your street, your school, your pet.</p>
              <p class="fragment">All of that is available. <b>Yesterday's session was the reason.</b></p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Multifactor</h2>
              <Code code={Factors} frame="code" lang="txt" title="Three Categories" showLineNumbers={false} />
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>The Distinction That Gets Tested</h2>
              <Code code={Two} frame="code" lang="txt" title="Count the Categories" showLineNumbers={false} />
              <p class="fragment"><b>Two of the same category is one factor.</b></p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Now the Other Question</h2>
              <p><b>Encryption</b> &mdash; encoding data to prevent unauthorized access.</p>
              <p class="fragment"><b>Decryption</b> &mdash; decoding it.</p>
              <p class="fragment">
                Authentication decides <i>who gets in</i>. Encryption decides
                <i> who can read it</i>.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Two Approaches</h2>
              <Code code={Encryption} frame="code" lang="txt" title="Symmetric and Public Key" showLineNumbers={false} />
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>The Problem Symmetric Has</h2>
              <p>One key, and both sides need it.</p>
              <p class="fragment"><b>So how do you send them the key?</b></p>
              <p class="fragment">Not over the channel you are trying to protect.</p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>What Public Key Solves</h2>
              <p>The <b>public</b> key is public. Anyone may have it. It only <b>encrypts</b>.</p>
              <p class="fragment">The <b>private</b> key never travels. It <b>decrypts</b>.</p>
              <p class="fragment">
                <b class="text-sn-theme">The sender does not need the receiver's private
                key to encrypt a message.</b> That is the whole trick.
              </p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>But Whose Public Key Is It?</h2>
              <p>Anyone can publish a key and claim it is somebody's.</p>
              <p class="fragment">Encryption does not answer that question.</p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Certificates Do</h2>
              <Code code={Certificates} frame="code" lang="txt" title="A Trust Model" showLineNumbers={false} />
              <p class="fragment">You trust the certificate because you trust who issued it.</p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Which Is Not Nothing</h2>
              <p><b>A trust model means the security rests on somebody's reputation</b>, not on mathematics.</p>
              <p class="fragment">If a certificate authority is compromised, so is everyone relying on it.</p>
              <p class="fragment">Redundancy had this shape too. <b>Session 68.</b></p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Today's Objectives</h2>
              <ul>
                <li>Explaining what makes a password strong, in the exam's terms</li>
                <li>Naming the three authentication categories and counting factors</li>
                <li>Defining encryption and decryption</li>
                <li>Distinguishing symmetric key from public key encryption</li>
                <li>Explaining what certificate authorities do</li>
              </ul>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Key Terms</h2>
              <div class="text-start">
                <dl>
                  <dt>Authentication</dt>
                  <dd>Measures protecting devices and information from unauthorized access.</dd>
                  <dt>Multifactor authentication</dt>
                  <dd>Access granted only after presenting evidence from at least two of: knowledge, possession, inherence.</dd>
                  <dt>Encryption / decryption</dt>
                  <dd>Encoding data to prevent unauthorized access, and decoding it.</dd>
                  <dt>Symmetric key</dt>
                  <dd>One key for both encryption and decryption.</dd>
                  <dt>Public key encryption</dt>
                  <dd>A public key encrypts; a private key decrypts.</dd>
                  <dt>Certificate authority</dt>
                  <dd>Issues digital certificates validating ownership of encryption keys, based on a trust model.</dd>
                </dl>
              </div>
            </section>
          </Presentation>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Build -->
        <section id="build">
          <h2>Today's Work</h2>

          <p>
            <b>No arithmetic anywhere on this page.</b> Specific mathematical procedures
            for encryption are outside the scope of this course and the exam.
          </p>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Task 1: Strong, or Just Long?</h3>

          <p>
            The exam's test: <b>easy for the user to remember, difficult for someone
            else to guess based on knowledge of that user.</b>
          </p>

          <p>For each, say <b>strong</b> or <b>weak</b>, and give the reason in the exam's terms.</p>

          <ol>
            <li>Your dog's name and the year you were born</li>
            <li>Your school's name spelled backwards</li>
            <li>Four unrelated words you chose at random</li>
            <li>A ten-character string of symbols you keep on a note in your bag</li>
            <li>Your street name with a symbol on the end</li>
          </ol>

          <p>
            <b>Item 4 satisfies half the test and fails the other half.</b> Say which
            half, and why that makes it a bad password in practice.
          </p>

          <p>
            <b class="text-sn-theme">Then connect it back:</b> for items 1, 2 and 5,
            name where somebody could find out the thing they would need to guess it.
            <b> Use yesterday's session.</b>
          </p>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Task 2: Count the Factors</h3>

          <p>
            For each, name the <b>category</b> of each piece of evidence, then say how
            many <b>factors</b> it is.
          </p>

          <table class="table table-sm align-middle mt-3">
            <thead><tr><th>The login asks for</th><th>Categories</th><th>How many factors</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td>A password, then a security question</td><td></td><td></td></tr>
              <tr><td>A password, then a code sent to your phone</td><td></td><td></td></tr>
              <tr><td>A fingerprint</td><td></td><td></td></tr>
              <tr><td>A fingerprint, then a PIN</td><td></td><td></td></tr>
              <tr><td>A password, then your mother's maiden name, then your first pet</td><td></td><td></td></tr>
              <tr><td>A physical key card, then a fingerprint</td><td></td><td></td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>

          <p>
            <b>Two rows ask for three pieces of evidence and are still not three
            factors.</b> Say which, and why.
          </p>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Task 3: Which Key, Which Job</h3>

          <p>
            <b>Ana wants to send Ben a message nobody else can read.</b> They have never
            met and have no shared secret.
          </p>

          <ol>
            <li><b>Could they use symmetric key encryption?</b> Say what problem they hit.</li>
            <li>
              With public key encryption: <b>whose key does Ana use, and is it the public
              or the private one?</b>
            </li>
            <li><b>Whose key decrypts the message, and which one?</b></li>
            <li>
              <b>Does Ana ever need Ben's private key?</b> Answer in one sentence, in the
              exam's words.
            </li>
            <li>
              Someone intercepts the message <b>and has Ben's public key.</b> Can they
              read it? Why not?
            </li>
          </ol>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Task 4: Whose Key Is It?</h3>

          <p>
            <b>A stranger sends Ana a public key claiming to be Ben's.</b> Encryption
            cannot tell her whether that is true.
          </p>

          <ol>
            <li><b>What goes wrong</b> if she uses it? Be specific about who reads the message.</li>
            <li><b>What does a certificate authority do</b> about this?</li>
            <li>
              The exam says certificates are <b>based on a trust model</b>. Explain what
              that phrase means for where the security actually rests.
            </li>
            <li>
              <b>What happens if a certificate authority is compromised?</b> Connect this
              to something from session 68.
            </li>
          </ol>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Checkpoint -->
        <section id="checkpoint">
          <h2>Checkpoint</h2>

          <ul>
            <li>What makes a password strong, in the exam's exact terms?</li>
            <li>Name the three categories, and how many are needed for multifactor.</li>
            <li>In public key encryption, which key encrypts and which decrypts?</li>
            <li>What do certificate authorities validate?</li>
            <li>What are you <b>not</b> expected to do with encryption on this exam?</li>
          </ul>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Reflection -->
        <section id="reflection">
          <h2>Reflection</h2>

          <p>Answer these before submitting your work.</p>

          <ol>
            <li>The exam defines a strong password as hard to guess <b>based on knowledge of that user</b>. Explain how session 75 makes that definition harder to satisfy than it sounds.</li>
            <li>Public key encryption lets two strangers exchange a secret without ever sharing one. Explain in your own words why that is surprising.</li>
            <li>Certificates rest on a trust model rather than on mathematics. Describe one risk that creates, and compare it to a single point of failure in a network.</li>
          </ol>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Submit -->
        <section id="submit">
          <h2>Submit</h2>

          <p>All four tasks, plus your reflection.</p>

          <h1
            class="text-center mt-5 text-sn-theme border border-primary slow-flash"
          >
            Activity Complete
          </h1>
        </section>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>
